{"name":"Gold Coast","hex":"#c78538","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Gold Coast","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A city and local government area in southeastern Queensland, Australia.","A region of West Africa on the coast of the Gulf of Guinea in the Atlantic Ocean west of the Volta River, now comprising the coast of Ghana.\n A former colony of Portugal in West Africa comprising its trading outposts along the Gold Coast.\n A former colony of the Netherlands in West Africa comprising its trading outposts along the Gold Coast.\n A former colony of Sweden in West Africa comprising its trading outposts along the Gold Coast.\n A former colony of Denmark in West Africa comprising its trading outposts along the Gold Coast.\n A former colony of Brandenburg and Prussia in West Africa comprising their trading outposts along the Gold Coast.\n  A former colony of the United Kingdom in West Africa comprising its trading outposts along the Gold Coast and its further northern conquests, now the independent state of Ghana."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Gold_Coast"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gold Coast (region)","blocks":[{"text":"The Gold Coast was the name for a region on the Gulf of Guinea in West Africa that was rich in gold, petroleum, and natural gas. This former region is now known as the country Ghana."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Zoom_Ahanta_Gold_Coast_1873.png/330px-Zoom_Ahanta_Gold_Coast_1873.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_Coast_(region)","wikidata":"Q1535868"},{"title":"Portuguese Gold Coast","blocks":[{"text":"The Portuguese Gold Coast was a Portuguese colony on the West African Gold Coast along the Gulf of Guinea."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Flag_of_Portugal_%281640%29.svg/330px-Flag_of_Portugal_%281640%29.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portuguese_Gold_Coast","wikidata":"Q770048"},{"title":"Gold Coast (British colony)","blocks":[{"text":"The Colony of the Gold Coast was a British Crown colony on the Gulf of Guinea in West Africa from 1821 until its independence as Ghana in 1957. The term Gold Coast may also refer collectively to the four separate jurisdictions administered by the Governor of the Gold Coast: the colony proper, Ashanti, the Northern Territories, and British Togoland. The name derives from the region's abundant gold resources, which attracted European traders, including the British."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Flag_of_the_Gold_Coast_%281877%E2%80%931957%29.svg/330px-Flag_of_the_Gold_Coast_%281877%E2%80%931957%29.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_Coast_(British_colony)","wikidata":"Q503623"},{"title":"Coast","blocks":[{"text":"A coast is the land next to the sea or the line that forms the boundary between the land and the ocean or a lake. Coasts are influenced by the topography of the surrounding landscape and by aquatic erosion, such as that caused by waves. The geological composition of rock and soil dictates the type of shore that is created. Earth has about 620,000.0 km (385,250.1 mi) of coastline."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Spring_Lake%2C_New_Jersey_Beach_at_Sunrise.jpg/330px-Spring_Lake%2C_New_Jersey_Beach_at_Sunrise.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coast","wikidata":"Q93352"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Stamp_Gold_Coast_Golden_Stool_with_GeorgeVI.jpg/330px-Stamp_Gold_Coast_Golden_Stool_with_GeorgeVI.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Stamp_Gold_Coast_Golden_Stool_with_GeorgeVI.jpg/330px-Stamp_Gold_Coast_Golden_Stool_with_GeorgeVI.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"1930s Stamp Gold Coast Golden Stool with George VI.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStamp_Gold_Coast_Golden_Stool_with_GeorgeVI.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/King_Asantehene_Osei_Tutu_II_of_Ashanti-Asanteman.jpg/330px-King_Asantehene_Osei_Tutu_II_of_Ashanti-Asanteman.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/King_Asantehene_Osei_Tutu_II_of_Ashanti-Asanteman.jpg/330px-King_Asantehene_Osei_Tutu_II_of_Ashanti-Asanteman.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Picture of an Akan monarch, King Asantehene Osei Tutu II","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKing_Asantehene_Osei_Tutu_II_of_Ashanti-Asanteman.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Elmina_Castle_%283587077829%29.jpg/500px-Elmina_Castle_%283587077829%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Elmina_Castle_%283587077829%29.jpg/1280px-Elmina_Castle_%283587077829%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Elmina_Castle_%283587077829%29.jpg/500px-Elmina_Castle_%283587077829%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Elmina Castle (São Jorge da Mina): the primary stronghold of the Portuguese in the Gold Coast, situated on a peninsula where the Benya River meets the Gulf of Guinea.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AElmina_Castle_(3587077829).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Amina.jpg/500px-A